    index: 18,
    part: "APPENDIX",
    title: "Tool Directory",
    subtitle: "Every Tool and Price in One Table",
    free: false,
    bodyHtml:
      "<p>Every tool named across Part 3, in one place, for whenever you need to compare pricing without flipping back through chapters. Every price was independently checked as of this edition — get a current quote before you commit, since this category's pricing moves often.</p><p>Two words repeat in nearly every row. A <b>tier</b> is which version of the software you're buying — most vendors sell a cheap Starter-type version and pricier versions above it with more features. A <b>seat</b> is one login for one person: a \"$99/seat/mo\" tool costs $99 for one recruiter, and roughly 3x that for a 3-person desk where each person needs their own login, unless the vendor bills the whole agency as one flat fee instead.</p><div class=\"callout info\"><div class=\"head\">What the price ranges mean, row cluster by row cluster</div><p><b>Business development:</b> Apollo is per-seat, as above; Crelate's BD pipeline is bundled into its ATS/CRM price. <b>Sourcing:</b> mostly $99-199/seat/mo — a solo recruiter pays that once, a 3-person desk pays roughly 3x it; Fetcher and hireEZ run higher, for agencies past the solo-desk stage. <b>Screening:</b> Manatal and Hirevire are per-seat; Paradox is a custom enterprise contract, not a realistic boutique-agency option. <b>Interview intelligence:</b> Metaview's free tier covers roughly 50 hires a year — enough that most small desks never hit its paid tiers. <b>Scheduling:</b> GoodTime is enterprise-contract pricing aimed at large internal teams, not a boutique agency's first purchase. <b>Reference checks:</b> typically billed per agency, not per seat, so a 3-person desk usually isn't paying 3x here — confirm on your own quote. <b>ATS/CRM:</b> per-seat, same math as Sourcing, and the highest-leverage line to get right early (Chapter 2, Chapter 9). <b>Rediscovery:</b> hireEZ's feature may be gated to its Enterprise tier — confirm before budgeting it as included free.</p></div><table><tr><th>Juncture</th><th>Tool</th><th>Price</th></tr><tr><td>Business development</td><td>Apollo</td><td>Free tier; $49-119/user/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Business development</td><td>Crelate BD Pipeline</td><td>Included with Crelate (see ATS/CRM)</td></tr><tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>Pin</td><td>Free tier; $99-179/seat/mo; custom Business</td></tr><tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>Juicebox</td><td>Free tier; $99-119/seat Starter; $199 Growth; custom Business</td></tr><tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>SeekOut</td><td>$149-179/mo Recruit Core (self-serve); enterprise custom</td></tr><tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>hireEZ</td><td>~$169-199/seat typical, no public list price</td></tr><tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>Fetcher</td><td>$379-849/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Screening</td><td>Manatal</td><td>$15/user Professional; $35 Enterprise; $55 Enterprise Plus</td></tr><tr><td>Screening</td><td>Hirevire</td><td>$39/mo Essentials; $99 Professional; $199 Agency</td></tr><tr><td>Screening</td><td>Paradox (Olivia)</td><td>$15,000-$100,000+/yr</td></tr><tr><td>Interview intelligence</td><td>Metaview</td><td>Free under ~50 hires/yr; $100/mo Pro; $300/mo Max</td></tr><tr><td>Scheduling</td><td>GoodTime</td><td>No public pricing; ~$15,000-$100,000+/yr</td></tr><tr><td>Reference checks</td><td>Xref</td><td>$70-485/mo by tier ($840/yr entry)</td></tr><tr><td>Reference checks</td><td>Checkr</td><td>From $29.99/report, effectively $55-85 after passthrough fees</td></tr><tr><td>Reference checks</td><td>Referoo / RefAssured</td><td>Flat-fee unlimited (contact for pricing)</td></tr><tr><td>ATS/CRM</td><td>Manatal</td><td>$15/$35/$55/user/mo</td></tr><tr><td>ATS/CRM</td><td>Recruit CRM</td><td>$95/$135/$215/user/mo</td></tr><tr><td>ATS/CRM</td><td>Crelate</td><td>$119/user Business; custom higher tiers</td></tr><tr><td>ATS/CRM</td><td>Loxo</td><td>$149/user Core (no AI); $199 Professional (AI); custom Enterprise</td></tr><tr><td>Rediscovery</td><td>hireEZ Rediscovery</td><td>Likely gated to Enterprise/add-on — confirm before budgeting as free</td></tr></table><h3>How to Use This Table</h3><p>Cross-reference the juncture column with Part 3 before you commit to anything — this table intentionally has no \"best for\" or \"how to pilot\" guidance, because that context lives in each tool's own chapter and matters more than the price alone. If a number confuses you on a vendor's own site, check which tier they're showing you, and whether it's per seat or for your whole agency.</p>",
